meta {
  name: Login
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/interface/main/main_screen.php?auth=login&site=default
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  authUser: {{username}}
  clearPass: {{password}}
  new_login_session_management: 1
  languageChoice: 1
}

assert {
  res.status: lt 400
  res.body: notContains login_screen.php?error=1
}

docs {
  Verified against a running instance: a successful login responds 302,
  redirecting to /interface/main/tabs/main.php?token_main=... A failed
  login (bad credentials) instead responds 200 with a small JS snippet that
  redirects the browser to /interface/login_screen.php?error=1 -- since
  both a successful login (when a client follows the redirect, as Bruno's
  CLI runner does by default) and a failed one (which is never a redirect
  at all) can return HTTP 200, the status code alone cannot distinguish
  them. The notContains assertion above is what actually catches bad
  credentials in this environment; the status check only guards against an
  outright server error.

  Bruno's cookie jar keeps the session cookie ("OpenEMR") this sets for
  every later request in the collection automatically -- no manual cookie
  handling needed.
}
